on a worcestershire farm  they harvest spring onions all day  in all weathers  this is hard graft paid by the bunch  and the workers here have come from all  over the world  but since brexit every one of them needs  a special visa  and this year workers have been in short  supply with claims of visa delays and  the war in ukraine leaving hundreds of  thousands of bunches in the ground  finding people now is becoming  increasingly difficult  at the beginning of may we were 40 short  of the people we should have here they  were all recruited they just hadn't got  the visas process  we lost three quarters of a million  bunches of spring onions with a value of  about a hundred thousand so overall  shortage labor cost us a quarter million  pounds in may  and it's not just onions this is what  asparagus looks like when you stop  picking it  in may they had to flatten the spears to  buy time as they waited for workers  and when they did arrive they weren't  enough they should have been harvesting  this asparagus until the third week of  june but the labor shortage meant they  couldn't keep up with growth and they  had to stop at the start of may letting  these plants grow to fern and leaving 45  000 kilos of asparagus worth 150 000  pounds in the ground  last year the government granted 30 000  seasonal work of visas of these nineteen  thousand six hundred and fourteen more  than sixty percent went to ukrainians  this year an extra ten thousand visas  have been granted taking the total to  forty thousand but the national farmers  union estimates seventy five thousand  are needed to meet demand  the government plans to end the scheme  altogether in 2024 but the conservative  chairman of the environment food and  rural affairs committee said that could  stop investment  it wants to be a permanent scheme if  you're planting a vineyard or building a  pack house you need to be sure that you  can have labour to come and do that work  in the into the future  this migration expert says cutting labor  may change what british farmers grow  you could imagine a scenario where the  scheme tapers down and  agriculture in the uk particularly  labor-intensive bits of the agricultural  industry like soft fruit  just become smaller  the government says it wants farmers to  produce more but british agriculture  can't grow if the labor force shrinks  paul kelso sky news in worcestershire
 

